        "type": "code",
        "language": "text",
        "title": "A useful sequence",
        "code": "1. Identify the visible regions.\n2. Identify repeated patterns.\n3. Identify independent interactions.\n4. Name meaningful sections.\n5. Build a component tree.\n6. Check which data must flow between branches.\n7. Only then begin implementation."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "This sequence prevents implementation details from determining the architecture too early."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "The Component Tree Is Not the DOM Tree"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "A component tree and a DOM tree are related, but they do not have to be identical. A component can return several nested DOM elements, and a component boundary can represent behavior or responsibility rather than a single visual element."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "language": "text",
        "title": "Component vs DOM",
        "code": "LessonCard component\n└── article\n    ├── h2\n    ├── p\n    ├── button\n    └── p"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The component gives the group a meaningful identity in the React architecture."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Use Design Repetition as Evidence"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "If a UI pattern appears repeatedly, that is evidence that the repeated structure may deserve a component. But the component should still represent a coherent concept."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Use Behavior as Evidence"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "A part of the UI with its own interaction often makes a natural component boundary. Search boxes, dialogs, tabs, and interactive cards are common examples."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Use Change as Evidence"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Another useful question is: which parts are likely to change independently? If navigation changes frequently while the lesson list changes for different reasons, separating them can make those changes easier to manage."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "language": "text",
        "title": "Three signals",
        "code": "Repeated\nor\nInteractive\nor\nConceptually independent\n\n→ candidate component boundary"
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "The Tree Should Help You Answer Ownership Questions"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "A good hierarchy makes later questions easier: where should the state live, which component should receive the data, and which child needs a callback?"
      },
